cles  so all of these are benefits of omega-3  fatty acids  now let us look at their effect in bit  more details  at a cellular level so every cell has  cell membrane  now these cell membrane are composed of  phospholipids  so omega-3 fatty acid can have profound  effect on these phospholipids  and they can be incorporated in a part  of these phospholipids  they can change membrane phospholipid  composition  and thereby membrane rigidity and  membrane signaling  let me give you an example so let's say  these phospholipids  are incorporating unsaturated fatty acid  chains  so they are not too much flexible and  the membrane is less fluid  but when an omega-3 fatty acid is  incorporated in the phospholipid then  due to the kink  present in their structure it is making  the whole structure  more flexible and it would prevent the  membrane to become  compact in other words membrane fluidity  is increasing that is in turn very  important for  signaling that is happening from the  membrane surface omega-3 fatty acid  incorporation is very  crucial other than that omega the  omega-3 for  fatty acids can directly bind to  the retinoic acid receptor retinoic acid  receptor is a very important  intracellular receptor that can leads to  several gene transcription so overall  omega-3 fatty acids might have  role to play in gene transcription  it turns out that whenever our body is  affected by some kind of pathogen may be  bacteria or viruses  our macrophages or dendritic cells  work like the first line of defense so  they  try they try to engulf the pathogen and  eliminate them  and macrophage and dendritic cells  secrete inflammatory cytokine which  is kind of protection towards the body  such as il  6 and il1 but too much production of  these inflammatory cytokines could be  damaging  so omega-3 fatty acids they prevent  secretion of inflammatory cytokines from  these cell types  omega-3 fatty acid actually regulates a  crucial transcription factor nf kappa b  not shown here